  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-7317",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $64:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/447dca25b6cd3e19abd115869dac18c66b6066a37ef6181b4ac68e478aafb0b8.png)\n\nTherefore, $64={2}^{6}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 64 & ={2}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $6$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${2}^{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5=960.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
